## Migration: ASP.NET Web Forms → ASP.NET Core 8

Your code uses two patterns that rely on `Thread.Abort` internally:

| Pattern | Framework Behavior | Core Replacement |
|---------|-------------------|-----------------|
| `Response.End()` | Throws `ThreadAbortException` to halt execution | Just return from the action |
| `Response.Redirect(url, true)` | Calls `Thread.Abort` to end the response | Return `Redirect()` result |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Swallows expected abort | Remove entirely |

---

### Migrated Code (ASP.NET Core 8)

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

public class ReportController : Controller
{
    // POST: /Report/Export
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Export()
    {
        var data = GenerateReport();

        // No Response.Clear/Write/End needed — just return a file result.
        // The framework handles Content-Disposition and Content-Type.
        var bytes = System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data);
        return File(bytes, "text/csv", "report.csv");
    }

    // POST: /Report/Redirect
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult RedirectAction()
    {
        if (!User.Identity?.IsAuthenticated ?? true)
        {
            // Return a redirect result — no thread abort, no endResponse param
            return Redirect("/login");
        }

        try
        {
            ProcessRequest();
            return Redirect("/success");
        }
        // No ThreadAbortException catch needed — redirects don't throw
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            LogError(ex);
            return Redirect("/error");
        }
    }

    private string GenerateReport() { /* ... */ }
    private void ProcessRequest() { /* ... */ }
    private void LogError(Exception ex) { /* ... */ }
}
```

---

### Key Pitfalls to Watch For

| Pitfall | Solution |
|---------|----------|
| **Code after `Response.End()` still executes** | In Web Forms, `Response.End()` aborted the thread so nothing ran after it. In Core, returning an `IActionResult` only works if you actually `return`. Any code after the return statement is dead code — but if you forget the `return`, execution continues. |
| **`Response.Redirect` in deeply nested methods** | If the redirect was called deep in the call stack (not directly in the action), you can't just `return Redirect()`. Instead, throw a custom exception or restructure to bubble up a result. |
| **`catch (ThreadAbortException)` hiding bugs** | These catch blocks sometimes masked real exceptions. Removing them may surface bugs that were previously swallowed. |
| **`User.Identity` is nullable in Core** | Always null-check: `User.Identity?.IsAuthenticated ?? false` |
| **No `Response.Write` in Core** | Use `IActionResult` return types (`File()`, `Content()`, `Ok()`) instead of writing directly to the response stream. |

---

### For Long-Running Exports (bonus)

If `GenerateReport()` is slow, use `HttpContext.RequestAborted` for cooperative cancellation:

```csharp
[HttpPost]
public async Task<IActionResult> Export(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    // ASP.NET Core automatically binds HttpContext.RequestAborted here
    var data = await GenerateReportAsync(cancellationToken);
    var bytes = System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data);
    return File(bytes, "text/csv", "report.csv");
}
```

This replaces the old pattern where `Response.End()` / `Thread.Abort` would kill a long-running request — now the client disconnecting cleanly cancels the token, and your code checks it cooperatively.
